WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.730
The Big Bang.

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.270
No space.

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.090
Total darkness.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:12.420
Nothing. Suddenly the universe sparks into
life.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:21.400
Really? Surely everyone knows you can't get
something from nothing.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:28.200
It's really the ultimate question How did the
universe come into being?

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.560
And the thing is, we don't want just
everything to come from nothing.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.170
It seems like a trick, but here we are.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.240
We exist. So something must have happened
and we just don't understand the physics of

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:39.930
it yet.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.450
This is one of the big open questions in
cosmology, the origin of the universe.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:51.270
And I think that people have variously said
stuff like, Oh, the universe has come from

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.340
nothing like, Ta.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.630
Da, now there's a universe.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:01:01.860
But remember, we don't have data about the
earliest moments of the universe.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.830
We don't know what was going on.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:12.540
We're struggling in the dark to get insight
into what may have happened before the Big

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.990
Bang. That period of apparent nothingness.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.990
Physicists look to empty space.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.690
But does empty really mean there's
absolutely nothing there?

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.310
It's not that there's something coming from
nothing, because that old fashioned idea of

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:34.360
nothing just doesn't apply to what we think
of as empty space.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.650
It turns out empty space is far from empty.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:46.450
The vacuum of space is really a writhing
sea, awash with charged quantum particles and

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.010
electromagnetic fields.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.630
The vacuum of space itself can be a very
dynamic thing.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:57.600
Matter can spontaneously appear out of the
vacuum and then spontaneously annihilate.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:02.170
The vacuum is full of particles and
antiparticles that are whizzing into

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.960
existence and then disappearing, colliding
with each other.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.780
Space is full of virtual particles popping in
and out of existence.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.390
And there's no question that they're real.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.370
Their effects are absolutely visible.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:16.390
We can see them.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.650
In extreme physics, things can get strange.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.650
Maybe nothing is something after all.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:35.260
If empty space contains particles that
apparently come from nothing, Could some sort

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.720
of similar process have triggered the Big
Bang?

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:49.690
The quantum vacuum itself can randomly,
spontaneously, without any input, just

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:56.860
have a lot of energy, perhaps enough energy
to spark something that we would call a big

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:57.460
bang.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:07.290
There are many speculative theories about the
origin of the universe, but is its sudden

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:11.070
appearance out of nothingness the only trick
it pulled off?

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.580
It also made matter from energy.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:20.040
The universe is full of galaxies, stars,
planets and comets.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.840
Where did they all come from?

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:26.790
According to the Big Bang narrative from one
tiny dot.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.680
When the universe began, there was actually
no room for matter at all.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:36.030
The temperature was so high, the spaces were
so compressed that matter couldn't exist.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.320
So how did the universe manage to become full
of matter?

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.200
In the primeval atom.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.850
There was no room for matter, but it was
crammed full of energy.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:55.470
And as Einstein tells us, all we need to
create matter is energy.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:03.070
According to E equals MC squared, energy and
matter are interchangeable.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:12.770
Einstein taught us with special relativity
that energy and matter are two sides of the

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.030
same coin.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.620
You can convert matter into energy by, say,
blowing something up.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.900
The most fearsome example of converting
matter into energy was the atomic bomb

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.820
developed in the 1940s.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.430
But with the big bang, this process was
reversed.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.940
Energy created matter.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:46.040
Matter that expanded out and out to fill a
whole universe.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:55.200
And in cosmic terms, the universe where
matter would develop grew remarkably fast.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:01.020
Right after the universe was born, it had an
unbelievable growth spurt, basically going

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.100
from being a toddler to a teenager in the
single tick of a clock.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.970
How could the universe get so big so quickly?

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:15.670
If we believe the Big Bang story, it appears
to have broken one of the most fundamental

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.080
laws of physics.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:24.520
Did the early universe really grow faster
than the speed of light?

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:35.040
Parts of the universe that are now separated
by 93 billion light years once had the same

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.330
cosmic zip code.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:44.070
To have expanded so quickly, inflation must
have broken one of the fundamental rules of

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:44.760
physics.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.970
We all know that one rule the universe sticks
to all the time is that nothing can travel

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.350
faster than the speed of light.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.700
So how can we possibly say that the universe
expanded faster than the speed of light?

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:01.440
But inflation says the universe expands into
nothingness.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.670
There was no outside of the universe.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.500
The universe was everything.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:15.970
So space itself was inflating and space can
move as fast as it wants.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:21.190
You can't go faster than the speed of light
through space, but space itself is allowed to

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:26.050
stretch and expand as fast as it wants, and
that's what our universe is doing.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:30.370
The idea of inflation smooths out the
universe in kind of a peculiar way.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:34.270
You can kind of think of it as having a
sheet with a lot of wrinkles in it.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:38.260
And if you take that sheet and snap it
really hard, those wrinkles very suddenly

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:39.190
flatten out.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:45.670
The theory of inflation is one of the
craziest sounding ideas in the history of

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:48.250
science. So crazy that it might just be
right.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.550
Inflation helps us to understand the
inexplicable.

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:55.550
But there's a problem.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.300
We don't know what triggered or powered
inflation.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:06.230
The inflationary universe idea imagines that
the universe was suffused with some kind of

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:11.330
ultra dense energy at early times, something
that pushed the universe apart.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.450
What caused there to be that hot, expanding
stuff?

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:19.710
We have to be humble and acknowledge that we
don't know for sure.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:25.680
But whatever started it, inflation was over
in a split second.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.990
It didn't last very long.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.350
And this is difficult to understand.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.810
How did inflation stop?

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.490
We don't know. We got nothing.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.910
Folks. Well, we got some things, but it's
tough.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:43.230
It's tough.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.000
But has the process of inflation really
stopped?

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:56.930
One radical theory proposes if the force
called inflation kick started the expansion

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.900
of one universe, then why not another?

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:02.420
And another and another?

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:08.150
Is inflation creating a whole series of new
universes?

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:11.810
Is there a multiverse?

